Here the trigram {{ill|Zhen|de|Zhen|ja|震}} is regarded as representing the emergence of yang, being assigned to the initial crescent of the month; subsequently, the moon was seen as evolving through the phases represented by {{ill|Dui|de|Dui|ja|兌|1=Dui (Bagua)|2=ja|3=兌|4=zh-yue|5=兌卦|6=ko|7=태 (괘)|8=fr|9=Duì|10=nl|11=Meer (trigram)|12=pt|13=Duì (I Ching)|14=zh-min-nan|15=Tōe-kòa|lt=Dui (兌)|qq=}}, {{ill|Qian|de|Qian|ja|乾}}, {{ill|Xun|de|Xun|ja|巽}}, {{ill|Gen|de|Gen|ja|艮}}, and {{ill|Kun|de|Kun|ja|坤}}. In this context the bright full moon represented by {{ill|Qian|de|Qian|ja|乾}} was associated with Jia, while the dark phase represented by {{ill|Kun|de|Kun|ja|坤}} was associated with Yi; the sun and moon were thought to meet as Ren and Gui from the north, before further entering the central positions of Wu and Ji.

== Heavenly Stems and Earthly Branches ==
Subsequently, the {{ill|Twelve Earthly Branches|1=Earthly Branches|lt=Twelve Earthly Branches|de|Zwölf Erdzweige|ja|十二支}} were also employed, and the stems and branches were assigned to each {{ill|Yao|de|Yao|ja|爻}} of the Eight Trigrams.

