'''Nakkou''' (納甲) refers to the allocation of {{ill|Ten Heavenly Stems|de|Zehn Himmelsstämme|ja|十干|12=draft}} to the {{ill|Eight Trigrams|de|Acht Trigramme|ja|八卦|12=draft}} in {{ill|I Ching|de|I Ging|ja|易|12=draft}}.

== Ten Heavenly Stems ==
The origin of Nakkou can be traced back to the method of xiangshu in the {{ill|Han Dynasty|de|Han-Dynastie|ja|漢代|12=draft}}, which interpreted the forms of the trigrams as expressions of the natural order of heaven and earth. It is said that {{ill|Jing Fang|de|Jing Fang|ja|京房|12=draft}} perfected Nakkou by incorporating the {{ill|Five Elements theory|de|Fünf-Elemente-Lehre|ja|五行思想|1=Wuxing (Chinese philosophy)|2=ja|3=五行思想|lt=Five Elements (Wuxing)|lt=Five Elements|lt_ja=五行|lt_de|Fünf Elemente|12=draft}} into the interpretation of the trigram images, and that it was subsequently transmitted by figures such as {{ill|Yufan|de|Yufan|ja|虞翻|12=draft}} of {{ill|Wu (Three Kingdoms)|de|Wu (Drei Königreiche)|ja|呉 (三国)|lt=Wu|lt_ja=呉|lt_de|Wu|12=draft}}. In this system the {{ill|Ten Heavenly Stems|de|Zehn Himmelsstämme|ja|十干|12=draft}} and the concepts of the {{ill|Five Elements theory|de|Fünf-Elemente-Lehre|ja|五行思想|1=Wuxing (Chinese philosophy)|2=ja|3=五行思想|lt=Five Elements (Wuxing)|lt=Five Elements|lt_ja=五行|lt_de|Fünf Elemente|12=draft}} and {{ill|Directions|de|Himmelsrichtungen|ja|方位|12=draft}} were assigned to the {{ill|Eight Trigrams|de|Acht Trigramme|ja|八卦|12=draft}}.

